INT. NEWSROOM - OVERNIGHT - EMPTY

Desks in rows. Dead monitors. A cleaning cart abandoned mid-aisle. An answering machine blinks on a corner desk, red.

A stairwell door opens.

LUCINDA VALE, forties, a long raincoat, a messenger bag, a flash drive between her fingers, slips in. She locks the door behind her. Crosses the room on tiptoe.

She stops at the corner desk. Presses the machine's button.

LUCINDA (V.O.)
(her recorded voice, tired)
Message one. Lucy, it's you. If you're hearing this, you're back at the paper. Don't go home. They'll be at the apartment.

Lucinda sits. Her hand covers her mouth.

LUCINDA (V.O.) (CONT'D)
The story's real. Every document. But they know about the drive.

She looks at the flash drive in her palm.

LUCINDA
(to herself)
Of course they do.

LUCINDA (V.O.)
Message two. Send it from the old terminal. The one with no network log.

LUCINDA
Clever girl.

The machine clicks off.

She rises. Crosses to a dusty terminal beneath a stack of old directories, blows off the dust, turns it on. It groans awake.

She plugs in the drive. A folder opens. Hundreds of files. She scrolls fast, eyes flicking.

LUCINDA (CONT'D)
(whispering)
There you are.

She selects a document and attaches it to an email. Her cursor hovers over SEND. Somewhere in the building, an elevator dings. She flinches, then steadies herself, and clicks.

A progress bar crawls.

LUCINDA (CONT'D)
Come on. Come on.

The bar hits ninety. Ninety-five. She pulls the drive free as it completes. The terminal chimes, softly: SENT.

Lucinda shuts her eyes, then pockets the drive, buttons her raincoat, and leaves the way she came.
